Let P be mean board power in watts over the generation window, P_0 the idle board power in
watts, and r the throughput in tokens per second. Energy per token isE_{\text{token}} \;=\; \frac{P}{r}

because watts are joules per second, and dividing joules per second by tokens per second leaves
joules per token. The marginal, or “above idle”, figure subtracts the power the card would have
drawn anyway:E^{\,\text{above idle}}_{\text{token}} \;=\; \frac{P - P_0}{r}

To turn either into watt-hours per thousand tokens, multiply by 1,000 and divide by 3,600.

Scientific notation writes a number as something between 1 and 10, multiplied by a power of ten.494{,}032{,}768 \;=\; 4.94032768 \times 10^{8}

It exists because model sizes are inconveniently large and weight values are inconveniently
small, and both are awkward to read with all their zeros in place.
